      },
      "lineNbr": 26,
      "author": {
        "id": 1120458
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-12-13T18:01:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "would any of these be better as a string? and if not, are they needed as separate byte arrays (would byte[] work better as AnqpData)?\n\nAlso, should any of these be fixed-length? (see b/207087196 - soon)",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 26,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 26,
        "endChar": 8
      },
      "revId": "e92db029dee5870e2a7199d62f6a68ba022906d5",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "73ecdb3e_1cd55055",
        "filename": "wifi/supplicant/aidl/android/hardware/wifi/supplicant/AnqpData.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 26,
      "author": {
        "id": 1882601
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-12-14T00:16:54Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It looks like the framework is expecting all of the fields to arrive as byte buffers. It then has a series of byte buffer parsers to convert each buffer into its equivalent in the framework. I think we need to keep these as individual byte arrays to avoid breaking the parsing logic.\n\nAlso, it looks like none of these fields are fixed length in the original HAL, but the fixed length feature would definitely be good for some other types like NetworkRequestEapSimUmtsAuthParams.",
      "parentUuid": "254e11e4_0cb276a9",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 26,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 26,
        "endChar": 8
      },
      "revId": "e92db029dee5870e2a7199d62f6a68ba022906d5",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1c247a79_2bae6f60",
        "filename": "wifi/supplicant/aidl/android/hardware/wifi/supplicant/AnqpData.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 26,
      "author": {
        "id": 1120458
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-12-14T18:31:53Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e I think we need to keep these as individual byte arrays to avoid breaking the parsing logic.\n\nWhat if these looked more like what the end result should be? We could have richer types here.",
      "parentUuid": "73ecdb3e_1cd55055",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 26,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 26,
        "endChar": 8
      },
      "revId": "e92db029dee5870e2a7199d62f6a68ba022906d5",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8c16051b_c3b2c7bd",
        "filename": "wifi/supplicant/aidl/android/hardware/wifi/supplicant/AnqpData.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 26,
      "author": {
        "id": 1882601
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-12-14T23:46:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I took a closer look at the parsers, and it looks like all of these buffers contain raw network data. For example, if you check out the comments at the top of the parser for venueName, the buffer contains 2 bytes of info data followed by a variable number of I18Name objects. Each I18Name object has a 1 byte length field, 3 byte language code, followed by a variable length string.\n\ncs.android.com/android/platform/superproject/+/master:packages/modules/Wifi/service/java/com/android/server/wifi/hotspot2/anqp/VenueNameElement.java;l\u003d30\n\nIt looks like the end data type is more complicated than a string for all of these. All of the parsers convert the byte buffer to some implementation of a framework AnqpElement object. Since the parsing logic and end data types already exist on the framework end, I think we should keep these all as byte buffers in the interface.",
      "parentUuid": "1c247a79_2bae6f60",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 26,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 26,
        "endChar": 8
      },
      "revId": "e92db029dee5870e2a7199d62f6a68ba022906d5",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
